        {
            "number": 5,
            "revision": "2bb6644c13a889a864f26c819ac48e00d8da847f",
            "parents": [
                "d40558216ad85e99f3c37614fc96e088241a4f72"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/38/88838/5",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Eric Yang",
                "email": "eric.yang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                "username": "fornchu110"
            },
            "createdOn": 1775042499,
            "author": {
                "name": "Eric Yang",
                "email": "eric.yang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                "username": "fornchu110"
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why make this change though?  Alignment with a software concept when this is a hardware design element in a hardware design description doesn't immediately follow to me.\n\nThese really are \"slots\" and not \"hosts\".  Especially since you seem to be changing the medusa board and not the compute card.  Right now you're working on a Yv4 design that has 8 slots each with a single host, but there are/were proposals for a storage variant that uses 2 or 4 slots per host, where the extra slots are populated by storage expansion boards."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Eric Yang",
                        "email": "eric.yang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "fornchu110"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for the feedback!. You're right that these are slots and the rename was the wrong approach.\n\nThe original motivation was to support multi-host power state monitoring in phosphor-pid-control([86857]). Given your point about topologies where the slot-to-host mapping is not one-to-one, would the right approach be to keep SlotId and introduce a separate HostId property alongside it as in [88869]? That way consuming services would use HostId for host power state lookups and SlotId for chassis power state lookups. This would also mean the host state handling in dbus-sensors [74073] needs an update to read HostId rather than SlotId.\n\nIf that sounds right, I'll abandon this change and activate [88869] with corresponding updates to [74073] and [86857].\n\n[74073]: https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/dbus-sensors/+/74073\n[86857]: https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-pid-control/+/86857\n[88869]: https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/entity-manager/+/88869"
                }
            ],
